question.fine_print:
This question will resolve based upon a ban being signed into law, rather than when it is implemented.&#x20;

"Cultivated meat" is [defined](https://gfi.org/science/the-science-of-cultivated-meat/) as genuine animal meat, including seafood and organs, produced by the cultivation of cells in bioreactors.&#x20;

For it to count, a law must fully prohibit the sale of cultivated meat within the state. Therefore laws such as Iowa's [Senate File 2391](https://www.thegazette.com/news/state/capitol-notebook-iowa-house-passes-bill-to-ban-mislabeling-alternative-meat-egg-products/article_b2d84289-99b6-5939-8399-845ce9b2423b.html), which regulates and restricts cultivated meat but does not ban the sale of it, would not count.&#x20;

An enacted law sunsetting (as in [Indiana](https://www.theindianalawyer.com/articles/trenton-morton-scrutinizing-indianas-rules-for-cultured-meat-labeling)) or being later overturned or enjoined (as was [attempted](https://ij.org/press-release/federal-judge-denies-preliminary-injunction-in-upside-foods-lawsuit-against-floridas-ban-on-cultivated-meat/) in Florida) will still count if it results in the prohibition of the sale of cultivated meat.

Partial bans (for example laws prohibiting the sale of cultivated meat to persons under 21 years of age) will not count.&#x20;

This question pertains to the sale of cultivated meat within a state, not to the purchase of it by individuals.

Please note that a trigger law (defined [here](https://www.law.cornell.edu/wex/trigger_laws)) only counts if the specified shift in controlling law occurs. An example of this is Louisiana SB 152, which [says](https://www.legis.la.gov/Legis/ViewDocument.aspx?d=1480467):&#x20;

> The provisions of this Part shall become effective only upon a final ruling by the United States Supreme Court holding that prohibitions on cell cultured food products do not violate the United States Constitution
